01:31:03.660 --> 01:31:06.300
there are x number of things that can go wrong.

1193
01:31:06.300 --> 01:31:08.940
And how do I think about where we are, where we're going,

1194
01:31:08.940 --> 01:31:11.940
and then where all of those sort of points are?

1195
01:31:11.940 --> 01:31:16.580
And I often describe this like fixing a lamp.

1196
01:31:16.580 --> 01:31:18.780
If a lamp is not working, the first question you should ask

1197
01:31:18.780 --> 01:31:21.700
is, is the light bulb working or is the bulb burnt out

1198
01:31:21.700 --> 01:31:22.940
and is it plugged in?

1199
01:31:22.940 --> 01:31:24.780
And if you can diagnose those two issues

1200
01:31:24.900 --> 01:31:27.620
and they're both working, then the issue

1201
01:31:27.620 --> 01:31:29.140
is somewhere in between those two.

1202
01:31:29.140 --> 01:31:32.220
And then your job as an engineer or someone

1203
01:31:32.220 --> 01:31:33.900
trying to fix this lamp, someone who's

1204
01:31:33.900 --> 01:31:35.700
trying to do it themselves, is to figure out

1205
01:31:35.700 --> 01:31:37.540
what point in that chain can go wrong.

